gined that it would be  so early on in his career we will never  know how much it affected him  although he did once hint at some  frustration  in a rare interview although prince  philip's military career was cut  shorter than he would have liked his  association with the armed forces  continued throughout his life amongst  his many honorary appointments across  the commonwealth  the duke of edinburgh became admiral of  the sea cadet corps  marsh of the royal air force and colonel  in chief of the british army cadet force  it was a sign of his duty to the armed  forces that his final  official solo engagement was to take the  salute from the royal marines  aged 96 and in the pouring rain  he was the captain general of the marine  corps for 64 years  a role that he cherished and the last  time the duke was seen in public  was in the summer of 2020 when he stood  down as colonel in chief of the rifles  and handed the title  to his daughter-in-law camilla it ended  in association with the regiment  that stretched all the way back to 1953.  we were all very proud of the juvenile  and he had served for six years through  the war  you know he had shown he had shown his  metal he was a brave officer  um he was an old-fashioned naval officer  you know he he was old-fashioned in his  view  and that had huge strengths we had a  whole navy full of old-fashioned naval  officers at one stage  you know and they had a great sense of  duty um he loved the navy itself  he knew about seamanship instinctively  um and those things were very important  that he and  and the navy realized that and the navy  loved him for it and he was seen as very  special  on his 90th birthday the queen appointed  prince philip  lord high admiral of the royal navy the  highest rank in the service  that he loved it was recognition of her  husband's lifelong dedication to the  navy  and the professional sacrifice that he  made to serve  her aleister bunkel sky news
 

